# Implement the device-neutral motion plan

## Scope and existing evidence

Historical structural evidence is not machine execution. Finite design
acceptance below does not grant hardware or device-plan authority.

Emit ordered pen-up and pen-down paths, speed, acceleration, optional pressure,
safe bounds, pauses, checkpoints, semantic origin, and estimated duration.

Current executable evidence now freezes the transport-neutral motion-plan
content
boundary without hardware authority. Ordered operations retain pen-up/pen-down
physical geometry, semantic origin, speed/acceleration intent, optional
pressure,
pauses/checkpoints, physical/writable-bound evidence, calibrated pen and live
capability assumptions, accepted revision identity, backend plan identity, and
optional estimated duration. The plan domain also owns the exact structural
operation kind (checkpoint, pause, pen-up, or pen-down) so downstream dry-run
validation does not redefine plan semantics. Path optimization, coordinate
calibration, plan identity computation, capability compilation, simulation,
vendor command projection, device I/O, and physical authorization remain open.

Current design evidence freezes Plan as a read-only derived application
capability bound to one accepted revision and live capability profile, with
deterministic inputs, blocking diagnostics, plan identity, read-only
cancellation/progress semantics, typed projection results, no file or device
side
effect, safe retry, derived invalidation, and browser/CLI/MCP parity. Backend
plan compilation remains open.

## Finite one-page device-neutral pen trajectory contract

**Inputs:** accepted revision, one admitted single-pen capability review,
selected calibrated pen/tool, physical page/writable bounds and ordered
semantic stroke centerlines. **Outputs:** one immutable read-only motion-plan
candidate with pen contact, speeds, pauses, checkpoints, semantic origin and
estimated duration, or an exact non-motion refusal. Planning never arms a
plotter, opens a USB port or sends an actuator command.

**Bounded A4 example:** use a `210 x 297 mm` page with writable rectangle
`x=20..190, y=20..277 mm`. A title line owner `t1` uses a pen-up travel to
`(30,40)` mm, pen-down from `(30,40)` to `(60,40)` mm at declared 20 mm/s,
then a pen-up travel to `(30,55)` and pen-down from `(30,55)` to `(65,55)`
for owner `t2`. Insert a checkpoint before the second pen-down and a
caller-authorized optional pause after it.

Record speed, acceleration cap
at 100 mm/s², pressure as `Unavailable` unless calibrated, all bounding
coordinates, exact accepted revision, plan identity and estimated duration
only when path/time model evidence is complete. No machine command is emitted.

**Contact/bounds rule:** pen-up travel cannot be mistaken for deposition;
pen-down must remain inside both page and calibrated writable rectangle.
Every operation carries its source identity or an explicit transport-only
reason, and stable order. A checkpoint records completed operation identity,
not merely a timestamp. A future duration estimate must include pen-up
travel, acceleration constraints, pauses and pen contact changes.

**Refusal example:** a title extends to x=194 mm, a requested pressure lacks
calibration, a required glyph has no admitted stroke, or the Live review
still contains a `Convert` item without accepted evidence. Refuse plan
compilation without clipping the stroke or inventing travel coordinates.
The pen cannot be started or armed by a read-only Plan result.

**Assumptions and limits:** typed plan content, provenance/operation kinds,
physical bounds, pause/checkpoint, revision and estimated-duration slots
exist. Path generation, calibration, time integration, simulator, vendor
projection, I/O and physical authorization are absent. The two-stroke case
is a theoretical path, not a tested device trajectory.

## Acceptance decision

- **Basis:** practical for typed plan and operation provenance; theoretical
  for actual trajectory compilation, 2026-11-02.
- **Delivered:** one A4 two-stroke sequence, pen-up/down/contact, speed,
  bounds, checkpoint and unsupported-feature refusal.
- **Not implemented by this acceptance:** executable planner, calibrated
  kinematics, duration estimator, simulator, vendor commands or arm/start.
